item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;When a Linux machine can’t reach the network, first find out what interfaces the kernel sees and whether they have addresses. A quick check can distinguish a missing device from one that’s simply down or not configured.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Start with a compact overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a fast scan, run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-br&lt;/span&gt; addr show
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You’ll get one line per interface, including its state and any assigned IP addresses. If you only need interface names and link state, use:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-br&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;link &lt;/span&gt;show
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To print just the names, without parsing command output:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;ls&lt;/span&gt; /sys/class/net
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That last command reads the kernel’s sysfs interface, so it’s handy in minimal environments and scripts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Choose the command based on what you’re checking
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;ip&lt;/code&gt; command, provided by &lt;code&gt;iproute2&lt;/code&gt;, is the usual starting point on modern Linux systems:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;link &lt;/span&gt;show
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This lists interfaces the kernel knows about, including ones that are down. To see addresses as well, use:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip addr show
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The shorter &lt;code&gt;ip a&lt;/code&gt; works too. For one device, pass its name:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip addr show enp0s3
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;An interface without an &lt;code&gt;inet&lt;/code&gt; line has no assigned IPv4 address. That can be normal: it might be down, unconfigured, or using IPv6 only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a quick list of only interfaces marked up, use:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-br&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;link &lt;/span&gt;show up
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or include addresses:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ip &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-br&lt;/span&gt; addr show up
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Be careful about what “up” tells you: it means the interface is administratively enabled, not necessarily that it has a working physical connection. An Ethernet interface can be up even when no cable is plugged in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Read the state in context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A full &lt;code&gt;ip link show&lt;/code&gt; entry might look like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;2: enp0s3: &amp;lt;BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP&amp;gt; mtu 1500 qdisc fq_codel state UP mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
link/ether 08:00:27:4a:1b:3e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The interface name is &lt;code&gt;enp0s3&lt;/code&gt;; &lt;code&gt;link/ether&lt;/code&gt; shows its MAC address. The flags and state describe its link status. Don’t treat the interface’s existence, administrative state, and physical connectivity as the same thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you need to check whether a particular Ethernet device detects a physical link, &lt;code&gt;ethtool&lt;/code&gt; can inspect that named device:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ethtool enp0s3
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Look for its link-detection information. &lt;code&gt;ethtool&lt;/code&gt; is for inspecting a device you already know about; use &lt;code&gt;ip link show&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;ls /sys/class/net&lt;/code&gt; to discover interface names.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


NetworkManager has a different view
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a system running NetworkManager, this command shows device type and connection status:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;nmcli device status
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It can also show the active saved connection profile, which &lt;code&gt;ip&lt;/code&gt; doesn’t track: &lt;code&gt;ip&lt;/code&gt; reports kernel-level interface details, while NetworkManager manages connection profiles. This distinction is useful on laptops and desktops, especially when checking Wi-Fi connections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every Linux system runs NetworkManager. Minimal servers may use another network configuration system, so &lt;code&gt;nmcli&lt;/code&gt; might be missing or have nothing useful to report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For wireless devices specifically, run &lt;code&gt;iw dev&lt;/code&gt; if the &lt;code&gt;iw&lt;/code&gt; utility is installed. For a complete list of wired, wireless, loopback, and virtual interfaces, use &lt;code&gt;ip link show&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Don’t assume the interface name
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You might see names such as &lt;code&gt;eth0&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;wlan0&lt;/code&gt;, or names such as &lt;code&gt;enp0s3&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ens33&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;wlp2s0&lt;/code&gt;. Different naming patterns are expected across containers, virtual machines, and physical machines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;lo&lt;/code&gt; is the loopback interface, used by a machine to communicate with itself. Names beginning with &lt;code&gt;en&lt;/code&gt; commonly identify Ethernet devices; names beginning with &lt;code&gt;wl&lt;/code&gt; commonly identify wireless devices. On many systems, predictable names are based on hardware or device location rather than the order devices happened to be detected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That’s why a configuration copied from another machine can refer to the wrong interface: confirm the actual name with &lt;code&gt;ip -br link show&lt;/code&gt; before changing network settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A practical troubleshooting sequence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a device appears to have a network problem, check in this order:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is the interface present?&lt;/strong&gt; Run &lt;code&gt;ip link show&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;ls /sys/class/net&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is it enabled?&lt;/strong&gt; Check the state with &lt;code&gt;ip -br link show&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Does it have an address?&lt;/strong&gt; Run &lt;code&gt;ip -br addr show&lt;/code&gt; and look for an &lt;code&gt;inet&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;inet6&lt;/code&gt; entry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Does the connection manager report a connection?&lt;/strong&gt; If NetworkManager is in use, run &lt;code&gt;nmcli device status&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Does the system have the route you expect?&lt;/strong&gt; Interface listings show devices and addresses, not which route the system uses. For the separate question of how to &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/find-ip-address-linux/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;inspect Linux IP addresses&lt;/a&gt;, check the addresses assigned to each interface; for routing, inspect the routing table.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If an expected interface is missing entirely, these commands tell you what the kernel currently sees; they don’t fix a hardware or driver detection problem. If the interface exists but has no address, focus next on its network configuration rather than assuming the device itself is absent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also keep the layers separate: an interface can be up without having an IP address, and an address alone doesn’t prove that a remote service is reachable. To investigate whether a service is listening locally, use tools such as &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/ss-command-linux/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ss&lt;/code&gt; to inspect sockets and ports&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For most checks, &lt;code&gt;ip -br addr show&lt;/code&gt; is the useful first command. Switch to &lt;code&gt;ip link show&lt;/code&gt; for full interface details, &lt;code&gt;ls /sys/class/net&lt;/code&gt; for names only, and &lt;code&gt;nmcli device status&lt;/code&gt; when you need NetworkManager’s connection state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I originally published a more detailed version of this guide on the &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/linux-list-network-interfaces/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SSHFlow blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm also building &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SSHFlow&lt;/a&gt; — an SSH client where every server gets its own workspace for terminals, SFTP, code, and databases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Repository: &lt;a href="https://github.com/albrescamily/semantic-image-search" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/albrescamily/semantic-image-search&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some people say we learn more from our mistakes… well, I can say I've learned a LOT by doing this project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was to create a system that receives an image or a text query and searches for matches in a database, similar to how Google Photos works. It seemed simple at first, but as I got into the mechanics, I kept running into problems that looked trivial on the surface and turned out to be much harder than I expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I will start by explaining the architecture, the design decisions I made, and why I made them. Also, I will analyze from a new perspective what I could have done better.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Setup and Infrastructure
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used LocalStack, which emulates AWS services locally. In this project, I use it mainly to simulate S3 object storage. I also run Qdrant as the vector database. Both LocalStack and Qdrant run in Docker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this prototype, everything runs on the same machine, so I access the services locally through their network APIs. I use Boto3 to communicate with LocalStack through the S3-compatible API, and I use the Qdrant Python client to communicate with Qdrant through its API. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn’t focus much on reproducing a production network topology. Even though I wanted to simulate a production-like system at a small scale, I kept the infrastructure local instead of deploying the services across multiple machines to simulate real systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhv73tt8ttb6z7c79gcth.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhv73tt8ttb6z7c79gcth.png" alt="Architecture Diagram" width="800" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
(Fig 1: Architecture Diagram)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;


Input Path
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the storage API, I created several routes to manage objects: upload, update, delete, and get/list operations. The upload endpoint is the main entry point for the ingestion pipeline. When a user uploads an image, the API layer orchestrates the storage, embedding, and indexing steps as one unit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The upload endpoint calls the embedding service and then the indexing service.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/upload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;upload_image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;UploadFile&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;File&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(...)):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;upload_to_s3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;embedding&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;embed_image_from_s3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;index_image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;embedding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Uploaded successfully!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The problem here is that the upload process is linear, and the upload route acts like an orchestrator. If a fault happens during one of these steps, it can cause inconsistencies between S3 and Qdrant. There is no guarantee that the whole upload flow will run without problems or that every service will always be available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also creates a scalability problem because the request depends on multiple operations being completed before returning a response. If one of these operations becomes slow or gets stuck, the request stays open for longer, consumes resources, and can increase the load on the system as more requests arrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also fundamentally an &lt;strong&gt;idempotency problem&lt;/strong&gt;. The system has no defined behavior for retries, duplicate uploads, duplicate IDs, or a step that hangs indefinitely. If a client retries after a timeout, does it create a duplicate object? Does it overwrite the original? Right now, the answer is undefined.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's another issue that's very important to address: deletions are not propagated safely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I want to delete an image, how will this operation be replicated so the corresponding vector is also removed from Qdrant? There is no guarantee.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The frontend should not be responsible for coordinating a delete operation across S3 and Qdrant. Ideally, it should only send something like &lt;code&gt;DELETE /images/{id}&lt;/code&gt;, and the backend should handle how this change is propagated across the system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since S3 and Qdrant are two independent systems, there is no shared transaction between these operations. If the image is deleted from S3 but the Qdrant operation fails, the vector can remain in the database even though the original object no longer exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is called the &lt;strong&gt;dual-write problem&lt;/strong&gt;. As the DDIA book discusses, writing related data to heterogeneous systems becomes difficult because we need some way to keep those changes consistent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One possible direction would be to change the architecture and make the propagation of these changes event-driven.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are a few approaches that could be considered:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two-Phase Commit (2PC):&lt;/strong&gt; The issue with using two-phase commit here is that neither S3 nor Qdrant exposes the kind of distributed transaction mechanism we would need to coordinate both writes, so this approach is not really practical for this system.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SAGA pattern:&lt;/strong&gt; A Saga could model the workflow as a sequence of operations and use compensating actions when something fails. This can reduce some coupling between services, but it also introduces eventual consistency and does not automatically solve reliable event publishing.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transactional Outbox pattern:&lt;/strong&gt; Probably the approach I like the most. Instead of directly trying to update every external system inside the same request, we can write the state change and an event into the same PostgreSQL transaction. Then, another process can publish or consume those events and update S3, Qdrant, or other services.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I really like the idea of the Transactional Outbox pattern, so I would probably choose this approach to address the consistency problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea of change data capture is also very interesting. PostgreSQL could act as the source of truth, while an outbox table records events that still need to be propagated. A worker or CDC mechanism could then read those changes and update the other systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Query Path
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the query enters the system, it is sent to the embedding service inside the &lt;code&gt;query_text&lt;/code&gt; route:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/query_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;input_query_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;query_embed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;embed_text_query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ann_search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query_embed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;images&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s3_client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate_presigned_url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;get_object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ExpiresIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3600&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;images&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;images&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I use the same image ID as the identifier for both the S3 object and the Qdrant vector. This gives me a direct mapping between the vector search result and the original image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using the same ID is useful, but it does not guarantee that both systems will stay consistent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the two stores get out of sync because some operation was not propagated correctly, a query can return a vector that has no corresponding object in S3. The endpoint also has no way to detect or recover from this situation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;generate_presigned_url&lt;/code&gt; call does not check if the object actually exists. It just creates a valid URL for that object key. So the frontend can receive the URL, try to load the image, and fail because the object is not there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Well, there are a lot of issues with my current project. I wanted to point out the most relevant ones I discovered, so there may be other problems I still don't know about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It was really fun building this project and interesting investigating the issues it has. Next time, I may refactor this project to build as event-driven to address those pain points&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Bonus
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Besides all the issues I addressed above, I also started thinking about the security side and how to keep the system secure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the things that came to my mind was user image metadata. Sometimes, images have sensitive information in their metadata, for example geolocation, that can put users at risk and should not be visible to other users. So, when an image enters the system, we need a process that removes this sensitive metadata from the version of the image that will be displayed. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the same time, I may still want to preserve the original image and its metadata in case the owner wants to download it again. The original version could stay private and accessible only to the owner, while the processed version would have sensitive metadata removed and would be used for search results and display.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks for reading these lessons learned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;-- Cami&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An Express health check page says the lesson-publishing API is unavailable. The Node.js &lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt; endpoint is failing while &lt;code&gt;/live&lt;/code&gt; still answers, and the on-call view shows the last readiness transition; it does not merely say "CPU high." That evidence is enough to ask whether learners lost access, whether only new publishing failed, and which release was serving traffic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: give an Express service three deliberately different endpoints: &lt;code&gt;/live&lt;/code&gt; proves the process can answer, &lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt; says it may receive traffic, and &lt;code&gt;/health&lt;/code&gt; returns a compact operator-facing summary. Record every readiness transition as a structured log, publish a state gauge plus transition and request counters, and keep an external regional probe. &lt;strong&gt;A green in-process endpoint is evidence about the process, not proof that a learner can reach it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This split makes incident reconstruction possible without turning a health handler into a second monitoring system. Keep the response small, bound every dependency check, and preserve the deployment and check results that explain a transition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should Express health check endpoints &lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;/live&lt;/code&gt; report?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start at the action the page requires. For an edtech publishing service, "API down" is too vague. The useful page identifies the affected public journey, the region that observed failure, the first failed probe, the current deployment, and whether the service removed itself from traffic. It links the operator to logs and metrics covering the same window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Work backward from there. The earlier signal should have been a readiness transition, not a liveness failure. A database pool that cannot acquire a connection within its budget may make publishing unsafe while the Node.js event loop remains alive. Readiness should flip to degraded and the load balancer should stop new requests. Restarting the process through a failed liveness check can amplify pressure on the same dependency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The endpoint contract is narrow:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Endpoint&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Consumer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Dependency work&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/live&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;orchestrator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Process is responsive&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;None beyond local process state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;load balancer or orchestrator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Safe to accept new traffic&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small, bounded critical checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/health&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;operators and internal monitors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Diagnostic summary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same cached check results, with context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Return &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; for healthy or ready and &lt;code&gt;503&lt;/code&gt; for degraded or unready. Do not put secrets, learner identifiers, SQL errors, or stack traces in any response. A useful body contains a status, a timestamp, a deployment identifier, and named check states. The detailed failure belongs in access-controlled logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One subtle rule matters during startup: liveness may become true before readiness.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During shutdown, readiness should become false before the process stops accepting work, leaving time for the load balancer to drain existing requests. The trade-off is visible: checks rich enough to diagnose a dependency can themselves add latency and load, while checks that inspect only process state miss the reason publishing is unsafe. Keep &lt;code&gt;/live&lt;/code&gt; local. Give &lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt; a small, explicitly chosen set of critical checks, cache their latest bounded results, and make &lt;code&gt;/health&lt;/code&gt; report that evidence without initiating a second round of expensive calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Instrument the transition, not the polling noise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Logging every successful probe at normal severity creates a wall of repetitive events and makes the incident harder to read. Access logs can retain probe requests at a sampled or low-verbosity level. The application should emit a structured event when state changes: &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;degraded&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;degraded&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt;, or a check reason changes materially.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each transition, retain &lt;code&gt;observed_at&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;service&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;environment&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;region&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;deployment_id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;previous_state&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;current_state&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;check&lt;/code&gt;, and a bounded &lt;code&gt;reason_code&lt;/code&gt;. Add a &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;span_id&lt;/code&gt; when the transition arose inside traced work; those fields permit correlation, but they do not create a distributed trace or a span tree by themselves. Never place a student email, assignment text, or access token in the event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metrics serve a different job. Publish a readiness gauge with values &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;, a monotonic transition counter, and request counters partitioned by endpoint and result. Avoid learner, course, request, or error-message labels. Those values have unbounded cardinality and make both storage and queries harder to operate. OpenTelemetry defines a gauge as a current-value measurement; that matches readiness state, while a counter fits transitions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following Go probe is intentionally outside the Express process. It checks one URL and submits a schema-validated log payload to Infrai through its plain REST API. The payload stays in a file because the discovery parameters for log search are undeclared and the supplied facts do not establish an ingest body shape; obtain the current request schema from public discovery, validate &lt;code&gt;event.json&lt;/code&gt; against it, and only then run this program. That avoids teaching a made-up contract. The code uses one API key from the environment, sets an explicit method, surfaces non-success bodies, and retries &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; responses with &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; or exponential backoff.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"bytes"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"encoding/json"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"fmt"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"io"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"net/http"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"net/url"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"os"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"strconv"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"strings"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ObservedAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"observed_at"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Target&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"target"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Region&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"region"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"status_code"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;LatencyMS&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int64&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"latency_ms"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Healthy&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"healthy"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"error,omitempty"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"HEALTH_URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"PROBE_REGION"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"INFRAI_BASE_URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;eventID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"EVENT_ID"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fprintln&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Stderr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"HEALTH_URL, PROBE_REGION, INFRAI_API_KEY, INFRAI_BASE_URL, and EVENT_ID are required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;parsedBase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parsedBase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Scheme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"https"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parsedBase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Host&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fprintln&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Stderr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"INFRAI_BASE_URL must be an absolute HTTPS URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WithTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewRequestWithContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;MethodGet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fprintln&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Stderr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

